
# OSWP Style Philosophy

## Default: work within Forty's existing vocabulary

Forty (main.css) defines a consistent visual language — font sizes,
colour palette, spacing rhythm, border treatment — across everything
it ships with. New elements should draw from that vocabulary by
default: reuse existing values (font-size steps, the navy/cyan
palette, hairline border style) rather than inventing fresh ones.
This keeps new work looking coherent with the rest of the site for
free, and keeps custom.css smaller — extending the theme's own
patterns instead of overriding them piece by piece.

Practical habit: check FORTY-ELEMENTS (the ported kitchen-sink
reference page) and the Forty CSS defaults reference doc *before*
writing a new rule from scratch. If Forty already has a class or
pattern for what you need, use it, or match its selector shape
closely enough that it inherits the same look automatically (see:
`#header .logo strong`, matched exactly to override cleanly).

## When Forty doesn't have an answer: compose, don't import

Some components genuinely don't exist in Forty's original set — it
was built as a landing/portfolio theme, not a WordPress content site.
The single-feature image caption is the first example: no
`<figcaption>` styling exists anywhere in main.css, because Forty
never had captions at all.

In that situation, the right move is to **compose a new rule from
Forty's existing vocabulary** — same font-size steps it already uses
elsewhere (e.g. `.8em`, matching buttons/menu items), same muted
colour convention (translucent white on navy, not a new hue), same
border treatment (hairline `rgba` border, matching `hr`/tables) —
rather than either:
- forcing an existing-but-wrong Forty class onto the new element, or
- reaching for an unrelated, unfamiliar visual style just because
  nothing "official" exists for this case.

The result should read as "the kind of caption Forty would have
shipped, if it had one" — not a foreign element bolted on.

## Override deliberately, not incidentally

Every rule in custom.css should be there for a reason you could
state out loud — either "this fixes a load-order/specificity issue
so Forty's own intended behaviour actually applies" (see: the earlier
CSS load-order bug), or "this is a considered, intentional departure
from Forty's default" (see: reversing the header title's
default/hover colours because the bright cyan felt too dominant).

Keeping overrides deliberate rather than incidental matters for two
reasons: it keeps custom.css auditable later (a future maintainer —
possibly not CLI-inclined — can tell at a glance which rules are
"restoring intent" vs "changing intent"), and it avoids the trap that
bit us earlier today, where several long-dormant rules (wrong load
order made them invisible) suddenly went live and changed the site's
appearance without anyone deciding that should happen.

## In short

1. Check Forty first.
2. If Forty has it — use it, or match its selector exactly to inherit it.
3. If Forty doesn't have it — compose from Forty's existing values, in Forty's own accent.
4. Every override should be a decision, not an accident.

## Feature image sizes

Three registered sizes, all hard-cropped (exact dimensions
guaranteed, source image cropped not stretched to fit):

| Key | Dimensions | Ratio | Use |
|---|---|---|---|
| `event-featured` | 800×450 | 16:9 | Original/default — events, general use |
| `oswp-square` | 800×800 | 1:1 | Square crop |
| `oswp-landscape` | 1067×800 | 4:3 | Landscape, less wide than 16:9 |

Registered in `functions.php`, inside the `after_setup_theme` hook,
via `add_image_size()`. Use in a template with
`the_post_thumbnail( 'key-name' )`.

Naming note: `event-featured` predates this being used on non-event
pages (e.g. static pages) — name is slightly stale but left as-is to
avoid touching working code. Worth a deliberate rename pass later if
it starts causing confusion, rather than renaming reactively now.


## How image ratio is actually produced

Not by different PHP templates, and not something deployment
changes — it's controlled by two things working together, both
already covered above:

1. **Registration** (`functions.php`, once, sitewide): each named
   size — `event-featured`, `oswp-square`, `oswp-landscape` — has a
   fixed width/height/crop rule attached to it via
   `add_image_size()`. This defines *what the size means*, globally,
   for the whole site.

2. **Generation** (WordPress core, automatic, per image): the moment
   an image is uploaded to the media library, WordPress reads every
   currently-registered size and generates a separate physical file
   for each one — e.g. one upload produces `photo.jpg`,
   `photo-800x450.jpg`, `photo-800x800.jpg`, `photo-1067x800.jpg` all
   sitting on disk. This happens once, at upload time, regardless of
   which (if any) template will ever display it.

Templates don't create crops — they just **choose which
already-generated file to display**, via
`the_post_thumbnail( 'size-name' )`. `page-ipsum.php` requesting
`event-featured` and `page-dolor.php` requesting `oswp-landscape`
aren't two different cropping mechanisms — they're two templates
pointing at two different pre-made files that already exist for the
same (or a different) uploaded image.

### The gotcha: registration timing vs. upload timing

Because generation happens at upload time, registering a *new* size
in `functions.php` does nothing for images already sitting in the
media library — those images were uploaded before that size existed,
so WordPress never generated a file for it. Symptom: pointing an
existing page at a newly-registered size shows nothing, or falls
back to another size.

Fix: `wp media regenerate --allow-root` — walks every attachment
already in the library and generates any sizes it's missing. Needed
once, any time a new size is added, to backfill old uploads. New
uploads from that point on get every registered size automatically,
no regenerate needed.

### Deployment

None of this is deployment-specific. Once live on the VPS, the exact
same two-step mechanism applies — sizes are still defined once in
`functions.php` (which travels with the theme/git repo), and
WordPress still generates files at upload time on whatever server is
actually running. The only deploy-related wrinkle: if the media
library itself (actual image files) is migrated from local to VPS
via a database/files copy rather than fresh uploads, run
`wp media regenerate --allow-root` once after migrating, for the
same reason as above — copied files exist, but sizes registered
*after* the original upload still need backfilling.
